"Draws equal-density curves over paired observations to summarize the shape of a two-dimensional distribution.\n\nUse when: Dense paired observations need shape comparison without individual mark clutter. Smooth contours are supported by a sufficiently large sample.\n\nAvoid when: Small samples or boundary constraints make the density estimate unreliable.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal and vertical positions encode the two measured variables. Contour geometry encodes equal estimated density, with levels explicitly defined.\n\nBuild: Estimate a two-dimensional density using a documented bandwidth and coordinate scaling. Select density thresholds or probability-mass regions and state which is used. Draw nonintersecting level curves with a labeled level key.\n\nCheck: Check density thresholds are not mislabeled as enclosed probability. Verify the plotted support respects physical boundaries. Inspect raw points or bins to confirm the contours do not invent structure.\n\nPitfalls: Bandwidth and axis scaling materially affect contours. Overlapping group contours may cross; prefer small multiples when line intersections obscure group membership.\n\nExample: Summarize synthetic engine speed versus vibration readings with labeled density contours and a light raw-point reference layer.\n\nAlternatives: 2D histogram, Scatterplot, Violin plot."
